  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nSeasonably cool weather continues across the region with the upper \nlevel ridge displaced to the southeast US by a large trough across \nthe West Coast. This large-scale trough features a strong upper low \ncurrently off the WA/OR coast. Consistent model guidance progs this \nfeature to maintain intensity, and slowly meander into the Pacific \nNorthwest into Saturday. Consequently, the cool mornings and below \nnormal high temperatures will persist into Saturday with a fairly \nstagnant synoptic pattern overhead. Slightly elevated flow under \nthis pattern will keep breezy west-southwesterly winds in place for \nthe mountains and into the adjacent deserts through Saturday as \nwell. Peak gusts generally 25-35 mph, locally up to 45 mph within \nmountain passes. \n\nOf most interest continues to be the influence the aforementioned \nupper level pattern has for Hurricane Marie and the degree of its \ninfluence to southern California. With the coalescence of model \nguidance featuring the strong upper low maintaining latitude near \nthe Pac NW and not pushing further south as guidance earlier in the \nweek suggested was possible, the upper ridge across the south-\ncentral US is able to extend a ridge axis from the Desert Southwest \ninto northern Mexico, helping steer Marie to the west and well out \nto sea. The only direct impacts from Hurricane Marie continues to be \nthe elevated swell/surf arriving this weekend...see the Beaches \nsection below for more details.\n\nLate Saturday into early next week...\n\nWhile the track for Hurricane Marie takes the storm out to sea and \nno direct impacts are anticipated, the larger-scale wave of mid \nlevel tropical moisture is set to move into the region during the \nday on Satuday. Combining this with the influence of the \nsouthwesterly flow aloft from the mid-latitude synoptic setup, \nwidespread chances of rainfall this weekend remains the highlight of \nthe forecast. There remains a great deal of spread in model guidance \nin how much, if any, rain falls, but confidence continues to slowly \nincrease that at least most of the area will see some accumulations. \nSunday looks to feature the greatest chances for rainfall. Within \nthis uncertainty, even the higher-end of ensembles don't suggest a \ntotal wash-out, with the 90th percentile favoring 0.4-0.5\" of total \nrainfall for the valleys and up to 0.5-1.0\" for the coastal \nslopes/mountains. Ensemble means, however, are closer to 0.2-0.3\". \nUntil subtle differences in the placement of the large upper low in \nthe PacNW and associated dynamical forcing are resolved and we \nstretch into the high resolution gudiance window, a fair amount of \nuncertainty in the precipitation forecast will linger.\n\nDespite the rainfall uncertainty, what is more certain is that the \ninflux of tropical moisture and its influence on the temperatures. \nWhile the addition of moisture and clouds will help keep high \ntemperatures near to below average into early next week, the cool \nmornings will trend warmer, with low temperatures returning back \nabove normal come Sunday morning.",
